  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1257 AM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA mostly zonal flow pattern with perhaps slight high pressure \ninfluences at the surface and aloft will last for one more day \nthrough Saturday despite longwave troughing causing positive \nvorticity advection across the region. Dry air will still dominate \nthe local atmosphere with enough stabilization to prevent shower \ndevelopment. Dew points are expected to fall into the 50s along with \ncooler daytime highs in the low 70s, leaving little to no momentum \nfor any potential convection on Saturday. Beyond Saturday though, \nthe pattern will begin to shift as the next low is expected to swing \nacross the Gulf as part of a larger longwave trough. Flow will shift \nmuch more southwesterly as this low advects across the Gulf, which \nwill enhance moisture advection back into South Florida. Latest \nguidance suggests PWATs rising above average to the 1.8-2.0\" range \nwith vorticity advection occurring around the mid-level trough. \n\nWhile this setup may not produce steady rainfall Sunday evening \nthrough the early week period next week, there will be potential for \nbouts of heavier rain and thunderstorms. Uncertainty still exists \nfor any potential impacts but we will monitor the situation as the \ndynamics and QPF become more clear in the next few days. The next\nfrontal boundary is then currently expected to clear out the \ndeeper moisture sometime towards the middle of next week, although\nuncertainty still is high with that as well.\n\nTemperatures will be in the low to mid 70s on Saturday and then rise \nback towards the upper 70s and low 80s for Sunday and through early \nnext week. Overnight lows range from the low to mid 50s for the Lake \nO region to the upper 50s and low 60s near the coasts on Sunday \nmorning. Lows then rise into the upper 60s and low 70s for Monday \nmorning through mid-week.",
